<h2>Disclosure first: Section 12(1) and the Sixth Schedule form</h2>
<p>Every person approached for appointment as arbitrator must disclose in writing, in the Sixth Schedule format, two kinds of circumstances: relationships — direct or indirect, past or present, financial, business, professional or of any other kind — with the parties, their counsel or the subject-matter of the dispute, which are likely to give rise to justifiable doubts as to independence or impartiality; and anything likely to affect the ability to devote sufficient time to the arbitration and finish it expeditiously. The Fifth Schedule supplies the working list of what counts for the first category. Disclosure is not a one-time event: the duty continues through the proceedings.</p>

<h2>Two schedules, two consequences</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Fifth Schedule — challengeable.</strong> Relationships and circumstances that may generate justifiable doubts: past engagements with a party, advisory ties, certain repeat appointments, interests in the outcome and the like. These do not disqualify automatically; they feed a challenge under Section 12(3), which the tribunal evaluates.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Seventh Schedule — ineligible.</strong> Closer relationships — employee, consultant, adviser or other business ties with a party, among others — attract Section 12(5): the person is ineligible to be appointed, whatever the contract says. The only cure is an express written waiver made after the disputes arose.</div>
</div>
<p>The grounds of challenge themselves are two, and only two: circumstances giving rise to justifiable doubts about independence or impartiality, or the arbitrator&rsquo;s lack of qualifications the parties agreed upon. A party who appointed, or participated in appointing, the arbitrator may challenge only for reasons it became aware of after the appointment was made — Section 12(4) shuts the door on tactical second thoughts.</p>

<h2>The Section 13 procedure and its fifteen-day clock</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Agree or default.</strong> Parties are free to agree a challenge procedure; failing agreement, the statutory default applies.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Fifteen days.</strong> The challenging party sends the tribunal a written statement of reasons within fifteen days of learning of the tribunal&rsquo;s constitution or of the disqualifying circumstances.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Tribunal decides.</strong> Unless the challenged arbitrator withdraws or the other party agrees to the challenge, the tribunal — including the challenged member — rules on it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Challenge fails? Proceed.</strong> The arbitration continues to award; the objection is preserved for a Section 34 setting-aside application.</div>
</div>
<p>This architecture is deliberate. It denies a disgruntled party the power to stall the reference by manufacturing bias objections, while ensuring that a genuine objection, raised in time and rejected, loses nothing: the setting-aside court examines it with the award. Where an award is set aside on such a challenge, the court may also decide whether the arbitrator is entitled to fees.</p>

<h2>The court route: Section 14 for de jure inability</h2>
<p>Seventh Schedule ineligibility is not merely a ground of challenge — it is a de jure inability to act, terminating the mandate by operation of law. Section 14 permits a party to apply to the court to decide the controversy over termination, which means an ineligible appointment can be dislodged without waiting years for an award. The same route serves where an arbitrator becomes de facto unable to act or fails to proceed without undue delay. A substitute arbitrator is then appointed under Section 15 by the rules that governed the original appointment.</p>

<h2>Practical counsel</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Read the disclosure the day it arrives, and diarise the fifteen days. The commonest fatality in challenge practice is the calendar, not the merits.</p>
  <p>Classify before you challenge: a Seventh Schedule relationship supports a Section 14 court application; a Fifth Schedule circumstance belongs before the tribunal under Section 13. Choosing the wrong door wastes months.</p>
  <p>Mind Section 12(5) waivers: only an express written agreement made after disputes arise cures ineligibility. Clauses signed at the contract stage cannot.</p>
  <p>Record everything: if the challenge fails and the matter proceeds, the Section 34 court will see only what the record preserves.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article explains the statutory framework in general terms for information. The assessment of any particular relationship or appointment requires advice on the specific facts and documents.</div>
